WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
1. Choose your workspace workflow
Use Cloud → Advanced → Icepack.
This run uses an editable workspace example and a short custom script for a 10 km square synthetic shelf.
This is separate from the canonical 02 Synthetic Ice Shelf spin-up.

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:28.000
2. Create an editable example
In Editor, enter a name and choose New example. This creates an owned workspace example.
The recorded example is pilot-icepack-script-1118; its file list is initially empty.
Setup pauses are omitted. Importing the source files follows.

3
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:46.875
3. Import the script and helper
Choose script files, select run.py and validate_dataset.py, then Import script files.
UTF-8 files can be up to 2 MiB each. Imported files are saved. Set run.py as the run target.
If files are seeded or already present, enable Replace existing script files only for the files you intend to overwrite. Importing does not launch a run.

4
00:00:46.875 --> 00:01:02.875
4. Reference the JSON configuration
Select icepack-shelf-small-v1.json in Datasets and choose Reference in example.
The script reads data/icepack-shelf-small-v1.json; staging copies the referenced file into that path.
This JSON describes a synthetic configuration. Gridded initial-field files are a separate workflow.

5
00:01:02.875 --> 00:01:20.875
5. Review the script’s science
Configuration: 10 km×10 km, 4×4 subdivisions, CG1 thickness/velocity, 253.15 K and zero accumulation.
Initial thickness tapers 500→300 m. The initial velocity guess increases 100→200 m/year along x.
The 200 m/year value is an initial guess, not a prescribed outflow boundary speed.

6
00:01:20.875 --> 00:01:36.875
6. Review and launch the native job
The validator checks the JSON schema, model, units and bounds before scientific imports.
The script runs a diagnostic solve, one 0.01-year prognostic step, then another diagnostic solve. Review readiness before launching.
The actual submitted script, helper and JSON hashes match the declared fixtures.

7
00:01:36.875 --> 00:01:52.875
7. Confirm actual completion
The native job succeeded with exit code 0.
Submission to Batch start: 220.087 s. AWS task: 48.192 s. The script’s own reported elapsed time is 15.6633 s.
These are separate timings. The setup/results recording gap is not solver time.

8
00:01:52.875 --> 00:02:06.875
8. Select the current run’s fields
Open Results and choose solution icepack, then thickness or velocity.
This run has 25 vertices and 32 triangles. The final fields are finite CG1 values, with positive thickness and zero accumulation.
No damage field or transient time axis is present in this one-step fixture.

9
00:02:06.875 --> 00:02:22.875
9. Inspect final thickness
This full figure is the exact downloaded archive member. Axes and thickness are in metres.
Final thickness ranges 300.023425–499.981468 m. The area-weighted mean is 400.006923 m.
Independent integration of the exported field agrees with the script metrics.

10
00:02:22.875 --> 00:02:38.875
10. Inspect velocity and metrics
Colour shows speed in metres/year; arrows show direction.
The domain RMS speed is 130.141951 m/year. It agrees with the script’s area-normalized speed_l2 metric.
This is actual native output. No separate PC/AWS or repeat-equality result is claimed.

11
00:02:38.875 --> 00:02:52.875
11. Save this run’s archive
Choose Download results and use the freshly generated HTTPS link for this current run.
The browser saved an 11-member ZIP with valid CRC: fields, mesh, figures, metrics and runtime/RNG provenance.
The generated link and downloaded archive were verified against this accepted 25-node run.

